From f62610cab9ca8641a684d7818d4516ecf7379500 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 18:13:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] stage 282: one mind - typed silence, an editable query, and
 the return path

Three things the policy could not express, joined to what already works.
The tape is 280's unchanged, so a difference against 280 is a difference
from these actions and nothing else. 37 actions, all lexical; no query
vector, no embedding of the question, and no label anywhere including
the teacher.

Silence is typed. One STOP served two situations with opposite
continuations - the witnesses contradict each other, and nothing was
found at all - and the features that separate them were already in the
state. STOP_CONFLICT and STOP_UNKNOWN make abstention a diagnosis, and
G_silence_is_typed checks the diagnosis against the situation that
produced it rather than counting refusals.

The query is an editable set of words, which 261 established it must
be. DROP_i removes one, ADD_i takes one from the passage just read, so
retrieval becomes part of the policy instead of a fixed function applied
to it. The typed silence is what makes the edit meaningful: UNKNOWN
means reformulate, CONFLICT means stop, and without the type there is no
way to tell those apart.

ASK_VALUE_i verifies by the return path. Counting witnesses was the only
check available; asking the tape about the value before answering with
it is a second one, and it is lexical too - Sleaford gives Lincolnshire,
then ask Lincolnshire and see whether anything leads back. It is a probe
that restores the candidate list, so it checks without navigating away
and the ANSWER indices stay meaningful. A plausible-but-wrong value
usually has no way back.

The teacher demonstrates all three, since an action it never takes is
one BC never shows. Verified on eight shapes: an empty list asks then
edits then gives up as UNKNOWN, equal support stops as CONFLICT, a lead
probes before answering, and a probe that finds no way back abstains.
--no-edit and --no-probe are the ablations.
